 any graph G, $\\begin{aligned} \\chi (G) \\ge conn(\\mathcal {N}(G))+3. \\end{aligned}$ (2) In this article we show that for a class of exponential graphs the bound given in (2) is tight. Further, we show that the neighborhood complexes of these exponential graphs are spheres up to homotopy. We were also able to find a class of exponential graphs, which are homotopy test graphs. In 1966, Hedetniemi conjectured that the chromatic number of the categori-cal product of two graphs is the minimum of the chromatic number of the factors. In 2019, Shitov [26] gave a counterexample to this conjecture. Let M(G) denotes the Mycielskian of a graph G. We show that, for any graph G containing $M(M(K_n))$ as a subgraph and for any graph H, if $\\chi (G \\times H) = n+1$, then $\\min \\{\\chi (G), \\chi (H)\\} = n+1$. Therefore, we enrich the family of graphs satisfying the Hedetniemi’s conjecture.\n\n\nORIGINAL PAPER\n\nNeighborhood Complexes, Homotopy Test Graphs\n\nand an Application to Coloring of Product Graphs\n\nSamir Shukla\n\n1\n\nReceived: 13 January 2020 / Revised: 2 April 2022 / Accepted: 7 April 2022 /\n\nPublished online: 13 May 2022\n\nÓ The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Japan KK, part of Springer Nature 2022\n\nAbstract\n\nThe neighborhood complex N ð G Þ of a graph G was introduced by L. Lova\n\n´sz in his\n\nproof of Kneser conjecture. He proved that for any graph G,\n\nv ð G Þconn ð N ð G ÞÞ þ 3:ð 2 Þ\n\nIn this article we show that for a class of exponential graphs the bound given in (2)\n\nis tight. Further, we show that the neighborhood complexes of these exponential\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1.1. **Internal partitions.** First, it is natural to consider the “opposite” of an external partition: in an *internal* partition, at least half of the neighbours of every red vertex are red, and at least half of the neighbours of every blue vertex are blue. These colourings correspond to locally *minimal* cuts, locally energy-minimising configurations in the *ferromagnetic* Ising model, or stable configurations of *majority dynamics* (which has been much more thoroughly studied than minority dynamics; see the survey in [50]). See the introduction of [9] for a review of the graph theory literature on internal and external partitions. (https://mkwn.github.io/PPRP.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; ** *For every* \\(d \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), *there are only finitely many* \\(d\\)-*regular graphs with no nontrivial internal partition. ... As far as we can tell, this conjecture first appeared in print in a paper of Ban and Linial [9], though it was previously posed in an open problem collection by DeVos [26].\n\nConjecture 1.1 is only known to hold for \\(d \\in \\{1,2,3,4,6\\}\\) (see [9, 55]). However, a weaker result is known to hold for all even \\(d\\): adapting Stiebitz’ ideas, it was proved by Linial and Louis [45] that if \\(d\\) is even, then a vanishingly small proportion of \\(n\\)-vertex \\(d\\)-regular graphs fail to have an internal partition (in the language of random graphs: a random \\(d\\)-regular graph \\(G_{\\mathrm{reg}}(n,d)\\) has a nontrivial internal partition whp\\(^2\\)).\n\n**Conjecture 1.1.** *For every* \\(d \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), *there are only finitely many* \\(d\\)-*regular graphs with no nontrivial internal partition.*\n\nAs far as we can tell, this conjecture first appeared in print in a paper of Ban and Linial [9], though it was previously posed in an open problem collection by DeVos [26].\n\nConjecture 1.1 is only known to hold for \\(d \\in \\{1,2,3,4,6\\}\\) (see [9, 55]). However, a weaker result is known to hold for all even \\(d\\): adapting Stiebitz’ ideas, it was proved by Linial and Louis [45] that if \\(d\\) is even, then a vanishingly small proportion of \\(n\\)-vertex \\(d\\)-regular graphs fail to have an internal partition (in the language of random graphs: a random \\(d\\)-regular graph \\(G_{\\mathrm{reg}}(n,d)\\) has a nontrivial internal partition whp\\(^2\\)).\n\n**1.2. Bisecions.** A *bisection* is a red-blue colouring where the numbers of red and blue vertices are equal (or differ by one, if the total number of vertices is odd). Although every graph has an external partition, some graphs (such as stars) do not have external bisections. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe finiteness conjecture for regular graphs without internal partitions | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/359132/the-finiteness-conjecture-for-regular-graphs-without-interna)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; 3 years old · traced to 2023 ... For every $d\\in\\mathbb{N}$d∈N, there are only finitely many $d$d-regular graphs with no nontrivial internal partition. Internal partitions are equivalent to locally stable two-colourings, and the conjecture asserts that regular graphs lacking such a nontrivial partition are exceptional. The source says it first appeared in print in Ban and Linial and had previously been posed by DeVos; its status is open.\n\n# The finiteness conjecture for regular graphs without internal partitions\n\n3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet $d\\text{?}$d? be a natural number, and let a nontrivial internal partition of a graph be a partition into two nonempty parts in which every vertex has at least half of its neighbours in the same part as itself. Finiteness conjecture for regular graphs without internal partitions. For every $d\\in\\mathbb{N}$d∈N, there are only finitely many $d$d-regular graphs with no nontrivial internal partition. Internal partitions are equivalent to locally stable two-colourings, and the conjecture asserts that regular graphs lacking such a nontrivial partition are exceptional. The source says it first appeared in print in Ban and Linial and had previously been posed by DeVos; its status is open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Michael Anastos, Oliver Cooley, Mihyun Kang and Matthew Kwan, “Partitioning problems via random processes”, arXiv:2307.06453 (2024).\n\ngraph-partitioningregular-graphs\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPARTITIONING PROBLEMS VIA RANDOM PROCESSES (https://tugraz.elsevierpure.com/ws/portalfiles/portal/76211366/2307.06453v1.pdf)\nciteturn4search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; As another example, the internal\npartition conjecture, due to DeVos and to Ban and Linial, states that for every d, all but finitely many ... It is a classical fact that every (finite1) graph has a red-blue colouring of its vertices, such that for ... Such red-blue colourings are often called external partitions, unfriendly partitions or ... CUT problem and is of fundamental importance in computer science and optimisation. ... Michael Anastos was supported by the European Union’s Horizon 2020 research and innovation programme under\nPARTITIONING PROBLEMS VIA RANDOM PROCESSES\nMICHAEL ANASTOS, OLIVER COOLEY, MIHYUN KANG, AND MATTHEW KWAN\nAbstract. There are a number of well-known problems and conjectures about partitioning graphs to\nsatisfy local constraints. For example, the majority colouring conjecture of Kreutzer, Oum, Seymour,\nvan der Zypen and Wood states that every directed graph has a 3-colouring such that for every vertex\nv, at most half of the out-neighbours of v have the same colour as v. As another example, the internal\npartition conjecture, due to DeVos and to Ban and Linial, states that for every d, all but finitely many\nd-regular graphs have a partition into two nonempty parts such that for every vertex v, at least half of\nthe neighbours of v lie in the same part as v.\nWe prove several results in this spirit: in particular, two of our results are that the majority colour-\ning conjecture holds for Erdős–Rényi random directed graphs (of any density), and that the internal\npartition conjecture holds if we permit a tiny number of “exceptional vertices”.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReceived: 25 July 2023 (https://research-explorer.ista.ac.at/download/18583/18639/2024_JournLondonMathSoc_Anastos.pdf)\nciteturn4search18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; internal partition conjecture, due to DeVos and to Ban\nand Linial, states that for every 𝑑, all but finitely many 𝑑- ... European Union’s Horizon 2020 research\nEmail: matthew.kwan@ist.ac.at\ninternal partition conjecture, due to DeVos and to Ban\nand Linial, states that for every 𝑑, all but finitely many 𝑑-\nregular graphs have a partition into two non-empty parts\nsuch that for every vertex 𝑣, at least half of the neigh-\nFunding information\nEuropean Union’s Horizon 2020 research\nand innovation; Marie Skłodowska-Curie,\nGrant/Award Number: 101034413; ERC,\nGrant/Award Number: 101076777;\nEuropean Union; Austrian Science Fund\nbours of 𝑣lie in the same part as 𝑣. We prove several\nresults in this spirit: in particular, two of our results are\nthat the majority colouring conjecture holds for Erdős–\nRényi random directed graphs (of any density), and\nthat the internal partition conjecture holds if we per-\nmit a tiny number of ‘exceptional vertices’. Our proofs\ninvolve a variety of techniques, including several differ-\nent methods to analyse random recolouring processes.\nOne highlight is a personality-changing scheme: we ‘for-\nget’ certain information based on the state of a Markov\nchain, giving us more independence to work with.\nM S C 2 02 0\n05C80, 05C15, 60C05 (primary)\n© 2024 The Author(s). Journal of the London Mathematical Society is copyright © London Mathematical Society. This is an open access\narticle under the terms of the